{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VeapcJgDVMAn8nsQHCqgFM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd109-dircolors\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/dircolors: Color Setup for ls\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  dircolors outputs shell commands to set the LS_COLORS environment variable, which controls\n6\t  file-type colorization in ls. macOS does not ship dircolors; the Go implementation must\n7\t  match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gdircolors, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte\n8\t  on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n9\t\n10\t  dircolors reads a color configuration database (either a file argument or built-in defaults)\n11\t  and translates it into a shell-evaluable assignment of LS_COLORS. The database format maps\n12\t  file types and extensions to ANSI color codes. dircolors requires pkg/sys for SIGPIPE\n13\t  handling.\n14\t\n15\tgoals:\n16\t  - G1: Output shell commands to set LS_COLORS from a color database, matching GNU dircolors output.\n17\t  - G2: Support Bourne shell (-b/--sh/--bourne-shell) and C shell (-c/--csh/--c-shell) output formats.\n18\t  - G3: Support printing the built-in default database (-p/--print-database).\n19\t  - G4: Parse the GNU dircolors database format including TERM entries, file type keywords, and extension mappings.\n20\t\n21\trequirements:\n22\t  R1:\n23\t    title: Shell output format\n24\t    items:\n25\t      - R1.1:\n26\t          text: |\n27\t            When invoked with -b, --sh, or --bourne-shell, must output Bourne shell commands\n28\t            to set and export LS_COLORS. The output format is:\n29\t            LS_COLORS='<value>';\n30\t            export LS_COLORS\n31\t            where <value> is the colon-separated list of type=code pairs derived from the\n32\t            database.\n33\t      - R1.2:\n34\t          text: |\n35\t            When invoked with -c, --csh, or --c-shell, must output C shell commands to set\n36\t            LS_COLORS. The output format is:\n37\t            setenv LS_COLORS '<value>'\n38\t            where <value> is the colon-separated list of type=code pairs.\n39\t      - R1.3:\n40\t          text: |\n41\t            When neither -b nor -c is given, must auto-detect the shell from the SHELL\n42\t            environment variable. If SHELL ends with \"csh\" (e.g., /bin/tcsh, /bin/csh), use\n43\t            C shell format. Otherwise use Bourne shell format. This matches GNU dircolors\n44\t            auto-detection behavior.\n45\t      - R1.4:\n46\t          text: |\n47\t            -b and -c are mutually exclusive. The last one on the command line takes\n48\t            precedence.\n49\t\n50\t  R2:\n51\t    title: Database parsing\n52\t    items:\n53\t      - R2.1:\n54\t          text: |\n55\t            Must parse the GNU dircolors database format. Lines beginning with # are comments.\n56\t            Blank lines are ignored. Each non-comment line has the form \"KEYWORD value\" where\n57\t            KEYWORD is a file type name (e.g., DIR, LINK, EXEC, FIFO, SOCK, BLK, CHR, ORPHAN,\n58\t            SETUID, SETGID, STICKY, OTHER_WRITABLE, STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE, CAPABILITY,\n59\t            MULTIHARDLINK) or a TERM entry, or a file extension (e.g., .tar, .gz, .jpg).\n60\t      - R2.2:\n61\t          text: |\n62\t            TERM lines specify terminal types for which the color database applies. If the\n63\t            current TERM environment variable does not match any TERM line in the database,\n64\t            must output an empty LS_COLORS value. When no TERM lines are present in the\n65\t            database, the database applies to all terminals.\n66\t      - R2.3:\n67\t          text: |\n68\t            File type keywords map to two-letter LS_COLORS codes: DIR=di, LINK=ln, EXEC=ex,\n69\t            FIFO=pi, SOCK=so, BLK=bd, CHR=cd, ORPHAN=or, SETUID=su, SETGID=sg, STICKY=st,\n70\t            OTHER_WRITABLE=ow, STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE=tw, CAPABILITY=ca, MULTIHARDLINK=mh,\n71\t            NORMAL=no, FILE=fi, RESET=rs, MISSING=mi, LEFT=lc, RIGHT=rc, END=ec.\n72\t            Extension entries (lines starting with . or *.) map to the full extension\n73\t            pattern in LS_COLORS (e.g., \"*.tar=01;31\").\n74\t      - R2.4:\n75\t          text: |\n76\t            When a filename argument is given, must read and parse that file as the color\n77\t            database. When no filename is given (and -p is not given), must use the built-in\n78\t            default database. The built-in defaults must match the output of\n79\t            gdircolors --print-database.\n80\t      - R2.5:\n81\t          text: |\n82\t            When \"-\" is given as the filename argument, must read the database from stdin.\n83\t\n84\t  R3:\n85\t    title: Print database, exit codes, and signal handling\n86\t    items:\n87\t      - R3.1:\n88\t          text: |\n89\t            -p or --print-database must print the built-in default color database to stdout\n90\t            in the dircolors database format (comments, TERM lines, keywords, extensions).\n91\t            The output must match gdircolors --print-database byte-for-byte.\n92\t      - R3.2:\n93\t          text: |\n94\t            -p is incompatible with a filename argument. If both are given, must print an\n95\t            error to stderr and exit 1.\n96\t      - R3.3:\n97\t          text: Must exit 0 on success.\n98\t      - R3.4:\n99\t          text: |\n100\t            Must exit 1 on error (invalid database syntax, file not found, incompatible\n101\t            options). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error including the\n102\t            filename and line number of the offending line in the database.\n103\t      - R3.5:\n104\t          text: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping dircolors output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message.\n105\t\n106\tnon_goals:\n107\t  - Custom color themes beyond the built-in GNU defaults are out of scope; dircolors reads whatever database is provided but ships only the GNU default.\n108\t  - Interactive editing of the color database is out of scope.\n109\t  - Validation of ANSI color code syntax within database values is out of scope; values are passed through verbatim.\n110\t  - COLORTERM environment variable handling is out of scope.\n111\t\n112\tacceptance_criteria:\n113\t  - id: AC1\n114\t    criterion: \"Bourne shell output -- gdircolors --sh and go run ./cmd/dircolors --sh produce identical LS_COLORS assignment output.\"\n115\t    traces:\n116\t      - R1.1\n117\t      - R1.3\n118\t      - R2.4\n119\t  - id: AC2\n120\t    criterion: \"C shell output -- gdircolors --csh and go run ./cmd/dircolors --csh produce identical setenv output.\"\n121\t    traces:\n122\t      - R1.2\n123\t      - R1.3\n124\t      - R2.4\n125\t  - id: AC3\n126\t    criterion: \"Print database -- gdircolors -p and go run ./cmd/dircolors -p produce identical default database output.\"\n127\t    traces:\n128\t      - R3.1\n129\t      - R3.2\n130\t  - id: AC4\n131\t    criterion: \"Custom database -- gdircolors --sh FILE and go run ./cmd/dircolors --sh FILE produce identical output when given the same custom database file.\"\n132\t    traces:\n133\t      - R2.1\n134\t      - R2.2\n135\t      - R2.3\n136\t      - R2.4\n137\t  - id: AC5\n138\t    criterion: \"Shell auto-detection -- with SHELL=/bin/bash, gdircolors and go run ./cmd/dircolors produce Bourne shell format. With SHELL=/bin/tcsh, both produce C shell format.\"\n139\t    traces:\n140\t      - R1.3\n141\t      - R1.4\n142\t  - id: AC6\n143\t    criterion: \"Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given both -p and a filename argument, and exits 1 for an invalid database file, matching gdircolors behavior.\"\n144\t    traces:\n145\t      - R3.2\n146\t      - R3.3\n147\t      - R3.4\n148\t      - R3.5\n149\t  - id: AC7\n150\t    criterion: \"cmd/dircolors compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n151\t    traces:\n152\t      - R1.1\n153\t      - R1.2\n154\t      - R1.3\n155\t      - R1.4\n156\t      - R2.1\n157\t      - R2.2\n158\t      - R2.3\n159\t      - R2.4\n160\t      - R2.5\n161\t      - R3.1\n162\t      - R3.2\n163\t      - R3.3\n164\t      - R3.4\n165\t      - R3.5\n166\t\n167\tpackage_contract:\n168\t  imports:\n169\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n170\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"887394ca-6926-4248-87fe-87134d407a7a","uuid":"db2b8213-01b0-4b98-8246-3348f8525282","timestamp":"2026-05-27T21:40:23.044Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5620/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd109-dircolors.yaml","content":"id: srd109-dircolors\ntitle: \"cmd/dircolors: Color Setup for ls\"\n\nproblem: |\n  dircolors outputs shell commands to set the LS_COLORS environment variable, which controls\n  file-type colorization in ls. macOS does not ship dircolors; the Go implementation must\n  match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gdircolors, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte\n  on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  dircolors reads a color configuration database (either a file argument or built-in defaults)\n  and translates it into a shell-evaluable assignment of LS_COLORS. The database format maps\n  file types and extensions to ANSI color codes. dircolors requires pkg/sys for SIGPIPE\n  handling.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Output shell commands to set LS_COLORS from a color database, matching GNU dircolors output.\n  - G2: Support Bourne shell (-b/--sh/--bourne-shell) and C shell (-c/--csh/--c-shell) output formats.\n  - G3: Support printing the built-in default database (-p/--print-database).\n  - G4: Parse the GNU dircolors database format including TERM entries, file type keywords, and extension mappings.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Shell output format\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: |\n            When invoked with -b, --sh, or --bourne-shell, must output Bourne shell commands\n            to set and export LS_COLORS. The output format is:\n            LS_COLORS='<value>';\n            export LS_COLORS\n            where <value> is the colon-separated list of type=code pairs derived from the\n            database.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: |\n            When invoked with -c, --csh, or --c-shell, must output C shell commands to set\n            LS_COLORS. The output format is:\n            setenv LS_COLORS '<value>'\n            where <value> is the colon-separated list of type=code pairs.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            When neither -b nor -c is given, must auto-detect the shell from the SHELL\n            environment variable. If SHELL ends with \"csh\" (e.g., /bin/tcsh, /bin/csh), use\n            C shell format. Otherwise use Bourne shell format. This matches GNU dircolors\n            auto-detection behavior.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: |\n            -b and -c are mutually exclusive. The last one on the command line takes\n            precedence.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Database parsing\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: |\n            Must parse the GNU dircolors database format. Lines beginning with # are comments.\n            Blank lines are ignored. Each non-comment line has the form \"KEYWORD value\" where\n            KEYWORD is a file type name (e.g., DIR, LINK, EXEC, FIFO, SOCK, BLK, CHR, ORPHAN,\n            SETUID, SETGID, STICKY, OTHER_WRITABLE, STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE, CAPABILITY,\n            MULTIHARDLINK) or a TERM entry, or a file extension (e.g., .tar, .gz, .jpg).\n      - R2.2:\n          text: |\n            TERM lines specify terminal types for which the color database applies. If the\n            current TERM environment variable does not match any TERM line in the database,\n            must output an empty LS_COLORS value. When no TERM lines are present in the\n            database, the database applies to all terminals.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: |\n            File type keywords map to two-letter LS_COLORS codes: DIR=di, LINK=ln, EXEC=ex,\n            FIFO=pi, SOCK=so, BLK=bd, CHR=cd, ORPHAN=or, SETUID=su, SETGID=sg, STICKY=st,\n            OTHER_WRITABLE=ow, STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE=tw, CAPABILITY=ca, MULTIHARDLINK=mh,\n            NORMAL=no, FILE=fi, RESET=rs, MISSING=mi, LEFT=lc, RIGHT=rc, END=ec.\n            Extension entries (lines starting with . or *.) map to the full extension\n            pattern in LS_COLORS (e.g., \"*.tar=01;31\").\n      - R2.4:\n          text: |\n            When a filename argument is given, must read and parse that file as the color\n            database. When no filename is given (and -p is not given), must use the built-in\n            default database. The built-in defaults must match the output of\n            gdircolors --print-database.\n      - R2.5:\n          text: |\n            When \"-\" is given as the filename argument, must read the database from stdin.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Print database, exit codes, and signal handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: |\n            -p or --print-database must print the built-in default color database to stdout\n            in the dircolors database format (comments, TERM lines, keywords, extensions).\n            The output must match gdircolors --print-database byte-for-byte.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: |\n            -p is incompatible with a filename argument. If both are given, must print an\n            error to stderr and exit 1.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: Must exit 0 on success.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: |\n            Must exit 1 on error (invalid database syntax, file not found, incompatible\n            options). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error including the\n            filename and line number of the offending line in the database.\n      - R3.5:\n          text: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping dircolors output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - Custom color themes beyond the built-in GNU defaults are out of scope; dircolors reads whatever database is provided but ships only the GNU default.\n  - Interactive editing of the color database is out of scope.\n  - Validation of ANSI color code syntax within database values is out of scope; values are passed through verbatim.\n  - COLORTERM environment variable handling is out of scope.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"Bourne shell output -- gdircolors --sh and go run ./cmd/dircolors --sh produce identical LS_COLORS assignment output.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.3\n      - R2.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"C shell output -- gdircolors --csh and go run ./cmd/dircolors --csh produce identical setenv output.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R2.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"Print database -- gdircolors -p and go run ./cmd/dircolors -p produce identical default database output.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"Custom database -- gdircolors --sh FILE and go run ./cmd/dircolors --sh FILE produce identical output when given the same custom database file.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Shell auto-detection -- with SHELL=/bin/bash, gdircolors and go run ./cmd/dircolors produce Bourne shell format. With SHELL=/bin/tcsh, both produce C shell format.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given both -p and a filename argument, and exits 1 for an invalid database file, matching gdircolors behavior.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n  - id: AC7\n    criterion: \"cmd/dircolors compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R2.5\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n\npackage_contract:\n  imports:\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n","numLines":170,"startLine":1,"totalLines":170}}}
