{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JN3atZqCGYUpmQ7TjvCtcC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand how `buildEnv` works. It starts from the current environment, sets `LC_ALL=C`, then overrides with entries from `tc.Env`. So if I set `Env: []string{\"SHELL=/bin/tcsh\"}`, it will override SHELL in the environment for both the Go and reference binaries.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement. Let me create the cmd/dircolors directory and write the implementation.\n\nKey observations from the reference output:\n1. The built-in database includes COLORTERM entries, which work like TERM entries\n2. The LS_COLORS value includes `do=01;35` for DOOR (not in SRD R2.3 but in the default database)\n\n\n3. Extension entries with `*` prefix (not `.`) like `*~` and `*#` are passed through directly\n4. Unrecognized keywords are silently ignored, but lines missing a second token produce errors\n5. TERM and COLORTERM matching uses glob patterns\n\nFor the implementation, I need to parse the full database and include the built-in defaults to match gdircolors output, make sure the keyword-to-code mapping includes DOOR even though it's not in the SRD, and handle COLORTERM entries the same way as TERM entries.\n\nWriting main.go...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":436,"cache_read_input_tokens":43148,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":436},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"887394ca-6926-4248-87fe-87134d407a7a","uuid":"55947f35-d99c-4b6c-a25d-d6e68d46db0d"}
